# Shell scripts must keep LF line endings. Committed with CRLF (which is what happens by default
# when they are authored on Windows with core.autocrlf=true), they fail the moment Linux runs
# them — "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M" — including in CI. The same goes for scripts that
# are sourced rather than executed: with CRLF, completions/qrshard.bash dies on the function
# definition — "syntax error near unexpected token `$'{\r''" — so sourcing it from ~/.bashrc
# registers no completions at all and prints that error in every new shell.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
